Defining Function 

C programming is based on functions. The C function definition has the            
following form: 

heading 
{ 
declarations (if any) 
statements 
} 

heading ​provides the ​name of the function, the​types of arguments (if any),             
and ​type of the returned value. The​must-have function that needs to run a C program                
is the main function, ​main()​. An example of a C program is shown in Listing 2-1. 


/* File:  hello.c */ 
#include <stdio.h> 

int main() 
{ 
printf(“Hello world!\n”); 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 2-1.​ hello.c 

The heading ​int main() gives the name of the function as​main​. The empty              
parentheses means that the function takes no arguments. The​int indicates that the             
function returns an integer value. The body of the function is a block containing two               
statements: 

{ 
printf(“Hello world!\n”); 
return 0; 
} 

Each statement ends with a semicolon. The value returned by​main() is a             
return code that indicates whether the program runs successfully. Usually, a return            
code of 0 indicates a successful run. 

