Statement 

A statement is a complete direction that instructs a computer to carry out some              
task. In C programming, a semicolon (;) is used to mark the end of a statement. This                 
rule is not applied to the preprocessor directives such as​#define and ​#include​. An              
example of  

x = 2*a + b;  

is an example of a statement. C programming is insensitive to the white spaces,              
including spacebar, tab, and newline. Thus, statements of 

x=2 * a+b; 

and 

 x  = 
2 * 
a + b; 

are all the same as the previous statement given above. If only a semicolon is given by                 
itself on a line, it is called a “​null statement​”, which does not perform any action.                
Some may call it, “​No Operation​” statement. 


Compound Statement 

A compound statement is a block of two or more statements in a pair of               
braces, {...}. It is also called “statement block”. An example of compound statements             
is given below. 

{ 
int x = 10, y = 20; 
int z = x + y; 
printf(“%d + %d = %d\n”, x, y, z); 
} 






