Field Width, Justification, and Precision 

We can insert a number right after a conversion specification (%) to format the              
string to be printed. For example, 

printf(“%6d”, 25); 

will pad four spaces before 25. Hence, the printed value is right-justified in its field.               
Left-justified string can be done by placing a minus sign in front of the number. For                
example, 

printf(“%-10s”, “Price”); 

prints ​Price​ followed by five spaces. 
The field width can be followed by a decimal point and a precision. For              
example, 

printf(“%7.3f”, 8.75); 

prints ​8.750​ right-justified in a seven-character field. Thus, 

printf(“%.3f”, 8.75) 

prints 8.750 in a five-character field. 

The following program in Listing 3-1 illustrates an example of how to use conversion              
specifications to justify printed strings. 


/* File:  justify.c */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  
{ 
printf("%-12s   %-7s\n\n", "Item", "Price"); 
printf("%-12s   %-7.2f\n", "Computer", 1999.95); 
printf("%-12s   %-7.2f\n", "Typewriter", 249.98); 
printf("%-12s   %-7.2f\n", "Paper", 5.99); 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 3-1. ​Using conversion specification for print 
25

char c; 
scanf(“%c”, &c); 
int x; 
scanf(“%d”, &x); 
float f; 
scanf(“%f”, &f); 
char str[10]; 
scanf(“%s”, str); 

From the example above, we can see that​scanf() takes the variable's address             
as its argument for all data types except the array of characters (string). In fact,               
variable ​str ​represents the beginning address of an array of characters. Alternatively,            
we can use ​&str[0] as the beginning address of the array. Listing 3-2 displays a               
program that receives data from​stdin ​(keyboard) and prints them with formatting on             
the computer screen. 
/* File:  input.c */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main() { 
char name[40]; 
int age; 
char grade; 
float gpa; 
printf("Enter your name: "); 
scanf("%s", name); 
printf("Enter your age: "); 
scanf("%d", &age); 

printf("Enter C programing grade: "); 
scanf(" %c", &grade);   ​//Make sure to precede a spacebar  
//in conversion specification 
printf("Enter your GPA: "); 
scanf("%f", &gpa); 

/*** Display the entered information ***/ 
printf("%10s: %s\n", "Name", name); 
printf("%10s: %d\n", "Age", age); 
printf("%10s: %c\n", "C Grade", grade); 
printf("%10s: %.2f\n","GPA", gpa);  
return 0;  
} 
Listing 3-2.​ Input with ​scanf() 
