More on Input Stream Functions 

C language provides other functions to read a single character from standard 
input stream (keyboard) as follows. 

int getc(FILE *stream); 

It reads a character at a time from an input stream and returns an integer value                
representing ASCII code, which will be converted to​char ​type. In case of failure,              
getc() returns ​EOF​. Since we need to read a character from the standard input stream               
(keyboard), ​stdin ​has to be passed as the argument stream on get ​getc()​.  

int getch(); 

Function ​getch() reads a single character from the keyboard. It returns ASCII            
code immediately without pressing the Enter key. However, the character that is            
entered will not appear on the computer screen. 

int getche(); 

Function ​getche() works as ​getch() does except that the character that is            
entered will appear on the computer screen. 

int getchar(); 

Function ​getchar()​ also reads a single character from the keyboard. It returns 
ASCII code after the Enter has been pressed. The entered character is displayed on the 
computer screen. 
The following statements illustrate how to use these functions, given variable 
c​ declared as char type. 

char c; 
c = getc(stdin); 

c = getch(); 

c = getche(); 

c = getchar(); 
29

Instead, if a string is to be received from a keyboard, function ​gets()​ is needed. 

char *gets(char *str); 

Function ​gets() reads a stream of data from the keyboard and puts it in a string                
variable (array of characters). On success, it returns the string received from the             
keyboard. Otherwise, it returns​NULL​. The following statements illustrate how to read            
a string to a variable using ​gets()​. 

char name[30]; 
gets(name); 

Listing 3-3 demonstrates how to use get and put functions to read a character              
from the keyboard and write it to the computer screen, while Listing 3-4 demonstrates              
how to work on string.  



/* File:  getc_putc.c */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main() 
{ 
char c; 
printf(“a : Add\n”); 
printf(“u : Update\n”); 
printf(“d : Delete\n”); 
printf(“Please select: ”); 
c =getch(); 

printf(“You select: ”); 
putchar(c); 

return 0; 
} 

