The ​if ​Statement 

The ​if​ statement has the form: 

if (expression) 
statement; 

The control expression is evaluated first. If the value is​true​, the controlled             
statement is then executed. If the control expression is​false​, the controlled statement             
is ignored. See an example: 

if (count > MAX_COUNT) 
printf(“Overflow\n”); 

An interesting example is given in Listing 4-1, on which indentations are used             
in trying to group statements and increasing readability of the program.  

/* File:  if.c */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  { 
int score = 49; 
if(score>=50) 
printf("You pass.\n"); //Controlled statement 
printf(“You are a good student.\n”); //Normal statement 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 4-1.​ ​if ​statement 

As mentioned earlier, statement​if requires only one controlled statement after           
the expression. Therefore, only the statement that prints out “​You pass.​” is skipped if              
the expression is evaluated as ​false​. “​You are a good student.​” will always be              
executed no matter what happens to the ​if ​statement. In this case, if multiple              
controlled statements need to be executed, they have to be grouped into a block as               
shown in the example below. 

if (count > MAX_COUNT) 
{ 
printf(“Overflow\n”); 
count = 0; 
} 
33

Thus, the program in Listing 4-1 should be fixed as shown in Listing 4-2 

/* File:  if_block.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  { 
int score = 49; 
if(score>=50)  { 
printf("You pass.\n");  
printf(“You are a good student.\n”);  
} 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 4-2.​ Statement ​if​ with block of statements 


From Listing 4-2, the ​if ​statement checks for an examination score that is             
greater than or equal to 50. If satisfied, this program will print out “​You pass​.”,               
followed by “​You are a good student​.”. Otherwise, it does nothing. What we can see               
from this is that this program ends its execution without doing anything. The​if              
statement is a one-way test. If we want to check for the alternative, we have to add                 
another ​if statement to do so. Listing 4-3 illustrates a two-way test, which solves the               
above problem. 

/* File:  multiple_if.c */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main() { 
int score = 50; 
if(score>=50)  { 
printf("You pass.\n");  
printf(“You are a good student.\n”);  
} 
if(score<50)  { 
printf("You fail.\n");  
printf(“Let’s study harder.\n”);  
} 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 4-3.​ Multiple ​if ​statement 
