The ​if-else​ Statement 

According to the problem in the previous section, C language actually           
provides two-way test for ​if ​statement as if-else statement Adding an ​else to ​if              
statement gives us the ​if-else​ statement as shown in the following. 

if (expression) 
statement-1 
else 
statement-2 

If the value of the control expression is evaluated as​true​, statement-1 is             
executed. If it is ​false​, statement-2 is executed as the following example: 

if (count < MAX_COUNT) 
count = count + 1; 
else { 
printf(“Overflow\n”); 
count = 0; 
}  

Thus, a better version of the program in Listing 4-3 is given in Listing 4-4. 

/* File:  if_else.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  
{ 
int score = 50; 
if(score>=50)  { 
printf("You pass.\n");  
printf(“You are a good student.\n”);  
} else  { 
printf("You fail.\n");  
printf(“Let’s study harder.\n”);  
} 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 4-4.​ ​if-else​ statement 


35

Nested ​if​ and​ if-else ​Statement 

The controlled statement in​else can itself be an​if or ​if-else statement. This              
results in nested ​if-else​ statements as shown in example below. 

if (expression-1) 
statement-1 
else if (expression-2) 
statement-2 
else if (expression-3) 
statement-3 
else 
statement-5 

In this case, the expression-1 is evaluated. If this is true, statement-1 will be              
executed. Then, the rest (from​else ​on) will be discarded. On the other hand, if the                
expression-1 is evaluated as false, its associated else is executed, at which its             
controlled statement is another​if-else statement. This requires the expression-2 to be            
evaluated before deciding whether statement-2 should be executed. This will go on            
and on until either will be satisfied. The following code in Listing 4-5 uses nested               
if-else​ to check the number of decimal digits in a positive integer ​n​. 

/* File:  nested_if.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main() { 
int n; 
printf(“Please enter number: ”); 
scanf(“%d”, &n); 
if (n < 10) 
printf(“One digit\n”); 
else if (n < 100) 
printf(“Two digits\n”); 
else if (n < 1000) 
printf(“Three digits\n”); 
else 
printf(“Four or more digits\n”); 
return 0; 
} 
Listing 4-5.​ Nested ​if ​statement 

Note:​ if multiple controlled statements are needed, they have to be in a block. 
