/* File:  switch.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  
{ 
switch(1)  { // Let’s try to change to some other number 
case 1: 
printf(“One\n”); 
case 2: 
printf(“Two\n”); 
default: 
printf(“Default\n”); 
} 

return 0; 
} 

Listing 4-6.​ Statement ​switch-case 


The problem of the program in Listing 4-6 is that once a case is matched, the                
corresponding state is then executed. Then, all the following statements in the            
following cases will also be executed. In fact, after executing the statements of the              
selected case, we want to leave the block and continue with the rest of the program.                
We accomplish this with a​break statement, which directs the computer to exit from a               
switch statement. Thus, the statements for each ​case ​should end with a ​break             
statement as illustrated in Lising 4-7. 











38






/* File:  switch_break.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  
{ 
int x=1;  // Let’s try to change to some other number 

switch(x)  { 
case 1: 
printf(“One\n”); 
break; 
case 2: 
printf(“Two\n”); 
break; 
default: 
printf(“Default\n”); 
break; // in fact, this break is not necessary 
} 

return 0; 
} 

Listing 4-7.​ Statement ​switch-case-break 


In Listing 4-7, after “One” has been printed, the execution will exit the​switch              
statement immediately. Sometimes, we may need​switch to match multiple cases. For            
instance, a program requires user to enter a single character for a menu selection. User               
may enter either a lowercase or uppercase character. Listing 4-8 demostrates how to             
use ​case-break to allow both lowercase and uppercase characters to represent the            
same option. 






39







/* File:  double_case.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  
{ 
char c; 

printf(“Enter a or b : ”); 
c = getch(); 

switch(c)  { 
case ‘a’: 
case ‘A’: 
printf(“You select a or A.\n”); 
break; 
case ‘b’: 
case ‘B’: 
printf(“You select b or B.\n”); 
break; 
default: 
printf(“You select something else.\n”); 
} 

return 0; 
} 

