The ​for ​Statement 

The ​for statement is suitable for iterations that have a definite number of             
loops. The ​for​ statement has the following form: 

for (exp-i; exp-t; exp-s) 
statement 

Expression ​exp-i assigns the control variable its initial value, ​exp-t tests           
whether the iterations should continue, and​exp-s steps the control variable from one             
value to the next. An example of using a​for statement to print a number from 1 to 10                   
is given below. 

for (n=1; n<=10; n=n+1) 
printf(“%3d”, n); 

From this statement,​exp-i (​n = 1​) assigns​n the initial value 1,​exp-t (​n <= 10​)                 
ensures that the value of​n will not exceed 10, and​exp-s (​n = n + 1​) increments the                   
value of ​n after each execution of the controlled statement (​printf()​). The above​for              
statement is equivalent to the following ​while​ statement: 

n = 1; 
while (n<=10)​  ​{ 
printf(“%3d”, n); 
n = n + 1; 
} 

which is in the form of: 

exp-i; 
while (exp-t) { 
statement 
exp-s; 
} 

Listing 5-3 demonstrates how to use a​for statement to compute a summation             
of integer numbers taken from ​stdin ​(keyboard). 




46

/* File:  sum.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main() { 
int number, sum = 0; 
for(n=1; n<=10; n=n+1) { 
scanf(“%d”, &number); 
sum = sum + number; 
} 
printf(“sum = %d\n”, sum); 
return 0; 
} 

Listing 5-3.​ Summation with ​for ​statement 



Compound Assignment Operator 

A compound assignment operator consists of a C operator followed by an            
equal sign (=), as in​+=, -=, *=​, and /=. Specifically, for the arithmetic operators, we                
have 

m += n is equivalent to m = m + n 
m -= n is equivalent to m = m - n 
m *= n is equivalent to m = m * n 
m /= n is equivalent to m = m / n  
m %= n is equivalent to m = m % n 

An example of how to use a compound assignment operator is given as 

m *= n+5; , which is grouped as m *= (n+5); 

The above statement is to add 5 to​n first, then multiply with​m​, and finally store the                  
result to the variable ​m​ itself​. 
From the example in Listing 5-3, 

sum = sum + number; 

could have been written more compactly and idiomatically as 

sum += number; 
47

Increment and Decrement Operators 

We already know that​n = n + 1;can be written more compactly as​n += 1;​.                  
Therefore, the following three expressions are equivalent: 

n = n + 1; 
n += 1; 
++n; 

Likewise, the following expressions are also equivalent: 

n = n - 1; 
n -= 1; 
--n; 

The increment and decrement operators can be used as​prefix operators (as in             
++n and ​--n​) or as ​postfix operators (as in​n++ and ​n--​). The prefix operator will                
increment or decrement the value of a variable and then return a new value to the                
variable as the value of expression. With the postfix operator, the value of the              
expression is the ​old value of the variable (the value before increment/decrement),            
then the value of the variable is incremented/decremented. For example, the           
statements 
int  n = 25; 

prefix operator postfix operator 
     m = ++n;      m = n++;  

The results from bother operators are not equivalent. Both will increment the            
value of​n but the value of​m in the left column,prefix operator​, will be 26 (increment                  
the value of​n first then assign to​m​), while the value of​m in the right column,​postfix                   
operator​, remains 25 (old value of​n​). However, after the assignment to​m has been               
executed, the value of ​n​ will become 26 (increment after). 

Note​: the assignment statements above can be grouped as 
m = (++n); 
m = (n++); 

,which yield the same results as the example above. 

An example of integer summation showing how to use both​++ (increment            
operator)​ and ​+= (compound assignment operator)​ is given in Listing 5.4. 
48



/* File:  new_sum.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main() { 
sum = 0; 
for(n = 1; n <= 10; ​n++​) { 
scanf(“%d”, &number); 
sum += number; 
} 
printf(“sum = %d\n”, sum); 
return 0; 
} 

