We can initialize an array when it is declared by listing the initial values for its                
elements in curly braces, as follows: 

int a[5] = {75, 25, 100, -45, 60}; 

An array with five elements has subscripts (array indexes) ranging from 0 to 4.              
The values assigned to the array ​a​ can be illustrated in figure 6-1: 









Figure 6-1.​ Array variable with values 


A declaration (with initialization) of  

int b[5] = {75, 25}; 

is equivalent to  

int b[5] = {75, 25, 0, 0, 0}; 

Subscripted array can be used in the same way as other typical variables, such as​x,                
score, discount,​ ​interest, etc​. For example, 

a[2] = 5; 

assigns 5 to element 2 (the third element) of ​a​, while 

n = a[i] – a[j]; 

assigns ​n​ with the difference value of the ​i-th​ and ​j-th ​of ​a​, and  

a[2*i – j]++; or ++a[2*i – j]; 

increments the value of the element (​2 * i) - j​. (​see postfix and prefix operators​) 

51


Constants represented by macros are frequently used in declaring and          
manipulating arrays. For example, if ​COUNT​ is defined by the directive 

#define     COUNT 1000 

then 

int c[COUNT]; 

declares variable ​c​ as an array of 1000 ​int​ objects. 

Subscripts (array index) for​c programming range from​0 through ​COUNT-1​.           
Typically, a ​for​ statement is used for processing all the elements of ​c​ as shown below. 

for(i = 0; i < COUNT; i++) { 
/* Process element c[i] */ 
} 

An example of setting all elements to ​0​ is given below 

for(i = 0; i < COUNT; i++)  
c[i] = 0; 

An equivalent implementation using ​while​ statement is given as: 

int i = 0; 
while(i < COUNT)  
c[i++] = 0; 


This above example uses postfix increment operator to increase the array           
index, ​i​ , by 1 after 0 has been assigned to the current ​i-th​ value of ​c​. 









