Array and ​for ​Statement 

The nature of the array variable is that it contains multiple values, at which a               
specific value can be accessed via its index. Accessing multiple consecutive values            
from an array is made possible by iterative statements. The most common way to              
access each value of an array variable is to use the​for statement, which can easily set                 
a number of loops it wants to iterate. Listing 6-1 illustrates how to assign a value to                 
each object of an array via​stdin (keyboard) using ​for statement. Subsequently, each             
value is printed out also using ​for​ statement. 



/* File:  array_for.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
int main()  
{ 
int i, num=10; 
int data[10]; 

for(i=0;i<10;i=i++)  
{ 
printf(“x[%d] : ”, i); 
scanf(“%d”, &data[i]; 
} 

for(i=0;i<10;i=i++)  
printf(“x[%d] = %d\n”, i, x[i]); 

return 0; 
} 

Listing 6-1.​ Array with ​for​ statement 









53


Listing 6-2 illustrates how to use a​for statement to read all values from an               
array of students’ scores. Then, all read values are used to compute a summation              
value and the average value respectively. 



/* File:  array_average.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
#define SIZE 10; 
int main()  
{ 
int score[SIZE] = { 4, 7, 9, 10, 6, 8, 5, 9, 8, 5 }; 
int i, sum=0; 
double avg; 

for(i=0; i<SIZE; i++)  
{ 
