Using ​const ​in Pointer Declaration 

In a pointer declaration, keyword​const can be used in two different places,             
with two different meanings. If​const is placed at the beginning of the declaration, the               
target object is declared constant. For example, if we declare 

const int *p; 

then the value of the target object ​*p cannot be changed with the assignment,              
increment, and decrement operators. Thus, statements such as 

*p = 100; 

and 

(*p)--; 

are ​invalid​. However, the use of​address-of operator to ​p is legal. For example, if​m                
and ​n​ are integer variables, then the statements 

p = &m; and p = &n; 

can be applied to ​p​. 

On the other hand, if​const precedes the pointer variable, then the value of​p               
(address of which ​p points to) now becomes constant. In this case,*p can be freely                
changed. For example, if we declare 

int *const p = &m; 

then ​p​ will retain its initial value of &​m​, Assignment and increment such as 

p = &n; 
p++; 

are ​not allowed. On the other hand, we can freely assign new value to​*p​, including                
increment and decrement ​*p​ as follow: 

*p = 25; 
*p += 3; 
(*p)++; 
