Pass by value versus pass by reference 

A function that receives a value of argument to its parameter is considered a              
pass by value​. All the examples given so far are the type of​pass by value functions​. In                  
this case, a copy of argument value is given to the parameter variable in function. Any                
changes to the parameter variable will not affect the argument variable in the calling              
program. A function that receives a reference of the argument variable is considered​a              
pass by reference ​. In this case, the calling program has to send the reference              
(address), instead of value, of its argument to the called function. Any changes to the               
parameter variable in the function will also take effect on the original argument             
variable in the calling program. Listing 9-5 illustrates a difference of using these two              
types. 


