Writing Data to a Text File 

In a text file, data can be written to by two main operations: writing one               
character at a time and writing one line at a time. The first relies on function​fputc() to                  
perform its operation, while the latter uses function​fputs() ​to perform its operation.             
The function prototype of​ fputc()​ is given below. 

int fputc(int c, FILE *stream);  

Function ​fputc() writes one character represented by (​unsigned char​) ​c to the            
opened file via file stream. If the operation performs successfully, it returns the             
character written to the file and advances the file pointer. If an error is encountered, it                
returns EOF. An example of how to write a character to a file is given in Listing 11-3. 


/* File:  putc2file.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
#include <stdlib.h> 
int main()  
{ 
FILE *fptr; 
char  c = ‘A’; 
fptr = fopen(“test.txt”, “r”); 
if(fptr == NULL) { 
printf(“Error: cannot open file.\n”); 
return EXIT_FAILURE; 
} 
fputc(c, fptr);  
fclose(fptr); 
return EXIT_SUCCESS; 
} 
Listing 11-3.​ Writing a character to file 


Listing 11-3 uses function​fputc() to write a character ‘​A​’ to a text file, test.txt.               
Since the file is opened in “​w​” mode, character ‘​A​’ will be placed at the beginning of                 
the file, which will write over an existing character if one exists. However, if the file                
is opened in append mode (​fopen()​with mode “​a​”), character ‘​A​’ will be placed at the                
end of the file. 

