Writing a Block of Data to a File 
A block of data may consist of textual or binary data or both. In this case, a                 
binary file is a better choice to be used for storing such data. C programming provides                
function ​fwrite() to write a block of data or structure data to a file. The function                
prototype of ​fwrite()​ is given below. 

size_t  fwrite(void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nelem, FILE *stream); 

The function accepts four arguments. The first is a​void ​pointer, which can be              
a pointer to any types of data including​user-defined data type such as​struct​. The               
second is the size of a block of data in bytes. The third is the number of blocks                  
(elements) to be written. And the final is the file stream to be written to. Upon                
successful write, the function returns a value of​nelem​. If it returns a value less than                
nelem​, then an error occurs. (​Note​: ​size_t ​is an alias name of​unsigned int​, which is                
already defined in the header file​stdio.h​.) Listing 11-5 illustrates an example of using              
fwrite()​ for writing structure data to a binary file. 
/* File:  struct2file.c  */ 
#include <stdio.h> 
typedef struct { 
char[50]  name; 
float  gpa; 
} std_gpa; 
int main() { 
std_gpa  student[10]; 
FILE *fptr = fopen(“test.bin”, “wb”); 
if(fptr == NULL) { 
printf(“Error: cannot open file.\n”); 
return 1; ​// return EXIT_FAILURE; 
} 
int i; 
for(i=0; i<10; i++) { 
printf(“Please enter student’s name: “); 
scanf(“%s”,_student[i].name); 
printf(“Please enter student’s GPA: “); 
scanf(“%f”, &student[i].gpa); 
} 
fwrite(student, sizeof(std_gpa), 10, fptr); 
fclose(fptr); 
return 0; 
} 
